---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import Flow from '../../components/Flow.astro';
import Shot from '../../components/Shot.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
import { fintrustFlow } from '../../data/diagrams';
import explained from '../../assets/projects/fintrust/fig-explained.jpg';
import notInDocuments from '../../assets/projects/fintrust/fig-not-in-documents.jpg';
import deadlines from '../../assets/projects/fintrust/fig-deadlines.jpg';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'fintrust')!;
---
<ProjectLayout
  project={p}
  title="Fintrust — regulation explained, every line sourced · Tejas More"
  facts={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Solo — product, retrieval, backend, frontend, infra' },
    { label: 'For', value: 'SEBI investment advisers & research analysts, NBFC compliance' },
    { label: 'Corpus', value: '7 RBI/SEBI documents · 267 pages · 703 sections' },
    { label: 'Answers', value: 'Cited to page and paragraph, in 2–5 seconds' },
  ]}>
  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">01 — The problem</p>
    <h2 data-split>Compliance means acting on 99-page circulars. Nobody has time to read them.</h2>
    <div class="tiles" data-stagger>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">1</span><h3>The rules arrive as long PDFs</h3><p>And the person who must follow them has a day job.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">2</span><h3>Most of it doesn’t apply to you</h3><p>You can’t tell which parts without reading all of it.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">3</span><h3>An unchecked AI statement is worse than none</h3><p>A fluent summary that’s wrong is a liability in compliance.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">4</span><h3>Obligations hide in prose</h3><p>And deadlines hide inside obligations.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">5</span><h3>Old rules are replaced, not deleted</h3><p>What changed matters as much as what’s new.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">6</span><h3>The advice line is a legal line</h3><p>Explaining regulation must never become investment advice.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">02 — How it works</p>
    <h2 data-split>Two paths, one database, a check after every model call.</h2>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        Ingestion runs offline: the documents are stored as immutable text, explained and mined for obligations, then checked.
        Questions run live: an advice check, hybrid retrieval in a single SQL query, a relevance gate, the model — and quote
        verification before anything reaches the screen. Postgres holds everything, including the vectors, so retrieval is a
        join and there’s no second store to keep in sync.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Flow diagram={fintrustFlow} />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">03 — Grounding</p>
    <div class="callout" data-reveal>
      <h3>How a statement earns its place on screen.</h3>
      <pre class="formula">the model must quote      "...not later than 21 days of the receipt of the grievance"
code finds the quote      at a character span in the immutable version text
each task adds checks     a deadline must appear inside its own quote
stored either way         <span class="ok">shown only if supported</span>

explanations of the 7 documents:  179 statements  →  <span class="ok">177 shown</span>  ·  <span class="no">2 withheld (quote not found)</span></pre>
      <p>
        Click any statement and the regulator’s own sentence lights up in the source. Spans point into immutable text, so a
        citation can never drift when a document is re-parsed — a section label can be wrong; the citation itself cannot.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Shot
      image={explained}
      alt="Fintrust explanation of the Master Circular for Investment Advisers, with numbered citations beside each sentence and the cited passages highlighted in the source text."
      caption="Every sentence of an explanation carries a numbered citation, and the passages it cites are highlighted in the regulator’s text."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">04 — The life of a question</p>
    <h2 data-split>“The documents don’t say” is a feature.</h2>
    <div class="numbers" data-stagger>
      <div><b>40 + 40</b><span>vector and keyword candidates</span></div>
      <div><b>k = 60</b><span>reciprocal rank fusion</span></div>
      <div><b>12</b><span>passages to the model</span></div>
      <div><b>0.32</b><span>relevance gate</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        “Should I buy Reliance shares now?” never costs a model call — a narrow pattern check declines advice before
        retrieval, and the model’s own flag declines it again if anything slips through. “What is the capital of France?”
        fails the relevance gate and gets “not in the documents” instead of a confident guess.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Shot
      image={notInDocuments}
      alt="Fintrust answering “What is the capital of France?” with “The documents don’t say”."
      caption="An off-topic question gets no confident guess — just “The documents don’t say”, and how to ask in the regulator’s words."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">05 — Deadlines</p>
    <h2 data-split>From a sentence to a date, without the model doing arithmetic.</h2>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        The model extracts obligations as cited records. Code checks that each deadline actually appears inside its own quote,
        and a deterministic generator turns them into <strong>132 dated deadlines</strong>, from three months back to two years
        ahead, on a board a compliance officer can actually open.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Shot
      image={deadlines}
      alt="Fintrust deadline board: 5 overdue, 3 due in the next 30 days, 1 dealt with, next due 30 Oct 2026."
      caption="The board: overdue, due in the next 30 days, later and dealt with — every card links back to its source."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">06 — What it taught</p>
    <h2 data-split>Defects no single test would have shown.</h2>
    <ol class="defects" data-stagger>
      <li><div><strong>Anyone could stall the whole API.</strong><p>Sign-in held one pooled connection while recording on a second; ten parallel failed sign-ins took all ten and deadlocked until timeout. Rule since: never await a second pooled connection while holding one.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Rate limits that only held one request at a time.</strong><p>Count-then-record let parallel requests through. Now admitted and recorded in one transaction under an advisory lock — proved with parallel requests.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>TLS that would have failed on launch day.</strong><p>Found by rehearsing the production database connection, not just the production code.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>A security test that passed because it tested nothing.</strong><p>It walked the routes, found none (they were nested), and passed. A security test should fail when its input is empty.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Found by photographing the product.</strong><p>Searching the library for “grievance” found nothing — search only read titles. It now searches the documents’ text.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Half a deploy.</strong><p>The web half went live and the API half didn’t. Check the live release, not the CI badge.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">07 — What isn’t finished</p>
    <h2 data-split>Honest limitations.</h2>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seven documents.</strong> Enough to prove the method; a real user needs every current circular for their registration.</li>
        <li><strong>Verified isn’t reviewed.</strong> A statement can quote the source accurately and still miss context; the human review queue isn’t built yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Extraction recall varies between runs</strong> (35 vs 22 obligations on the same circular) — every one kept was verified, which is why production loads a verified snapshot.</li>
        <li><strong>Tenant isolation is in the application’s queries,</strong> not yet in Postgres row-level security.</li>
        <li><strong>Free tiers:</strong> a sleeping API, one region, no staging, and one model vendor.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">08 — Built to be checked</p>
    <h2 data-split>The unglamorous parts.</h2>
    <div class="numbers" data-stagger>
      <div><b>225</b><span>tests against a real Postgres</span></div>
      <div><b>WCAG 2.1 AA</b><span>on every page</span></div>
      <div><b>100%</b><span>of AI calls logged — model, prompt version, tokens, latency</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
